ther way  around we've factored it the other way  around and now thinking of the the  coefficient of of radio as changing as a  function of tv  and it'll be 29 plus 1.1 times tv units  so you can make either of those  interpretations when you put an  interaction in the model  sometimes it's the case that an  interaction term is a very small p-value  but the associated main effects in this  case tv and radio do not  but when we put an interaction in we  tend to leave in the main effects and we  call this the hierarchy principle  and so there it stated if we put in an  inter interaction we put in the main  effects even if the p-values associated  with the coefficients are not  significant  so why do we do this  it's just that interactions are hard to  interpret in a model without main  effects their meaning actually changes  and and so  it's it's just generally not a good  practice  um another way of saying this is that  the interaction term also contains main  effects  um  even if you if you put the model with no  main effect terms so it just becomes  more cumbersome to interpret  now what if we want to put in  interactions between a qualitative and a  quantitative variable  turns out that's actually easier to  interpret  and and we'll see we'll see that now so  let's look at the credit card data set  again  and let's suppose we're going to predict  balance as as before  and we're going to use income which is  the quantitative variable and student  status which is qualitative and so we'll  have a dummy variable for student  which will be one if the person's a  student otherwise a zero  so without an interaction the model  looks  like  like this  and we see we've got an intercept we've  got a coefficient for income  and then we're going to have beta 2 if  the person is a student and 0 if the  person's not a student  and another way to write that is  a coefficient on income  and we've just lumped together the  intercept and the dummy variable for  student  and we can t